# Citation metadata of libopensps (https://citation-file-format.github.io). The FSPS papers are
# listed under references: please cite them in any work that uses this library.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use libopensps, please cite the FSPS papers listed under references
  (Conroy, Gunn & White 2009; Conroy & Gunn 2010), as well as this software.
type: software
title: "libopensps: a modernized FSPS stellar population synthesis library"
abstract: >-
  libopensps is a library for stellar population synthesis derived from FSPS
  (Flexible Stellar Population Synthesis) by Charlie Conroy and contributors:
  a thread-safe Fortran 2008 code with a stable C API, batched composite
  populations for fitting, FP32 kernels and GPU offload.
authors:
  - family-names: Mathews
    given-names: Elijah
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/TODO"
repository-code: "https://github.com/elijahmathews/libopensps"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - stellar population synthesis
  - galaxies
  - spectral energy distributions
  - astronomy
# TODO at the first release:
# version: "TODO"
# date-released: "TODO"
# doi: "TODO (archive DOI of the release, e.g. Zenodo)"
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      The Propagation of Uncertainties in Stellar Population Synthesis Modeling.
      I. The Relevance of Uncertain Aspects of Stellar Evolution and the Initial
      Mass Function to the Derived Physical Properties of Galaxies
    authors:
      - family-names: Conroy
        given-names: Charlie
      - family-names: Gunn
        given-names: James E.
      - family-names: White
        given-names: Martin
    journal: The Astrophysical Journal
    volume: 699
    issue: 1
    start: 486
    end: 506
    year: 2009
    doi: 10.1088/0004-637X/699/1/486
  - type: article
    title: >-
      The Propagation of Uncertainties in Stellar Population Synthesis Modeling.
      III. Model Calibration, Comparison, and Evaluation
    authors:
      - family-names: Conroy
        given-names: Charlie
      - family-names: Gunn
        given-names: James E.
    journal: The Astrophysical Journal
    volume: 712
    issue: 2
    start: 833
    end: 857
    year: 2010
    doi: 10.1088/0004-637X/712/2/833
